22:01:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s a great question—and the idea you’re referring to is one of the most common myths in taste science. The short answer is: **we don’t.** You can taste sweet, bitter, sour, salty, and umami all over your tongue. The famous “tongue map” that shows sweet at the tip and bitter at the back is wrong, and science has known this for decades. ### How the myth started The map comes from a 1901 paper by German scientist D.P. Hänig. He measured how sensitive different parts of the tongue were to the four basic tastes known at the time (sweet, sour, salty, bitter). He found *small* differences in thresholds—for example, the tip was *slightly* more sensitive to sweet than other areas, and the back was *slightly* more sensitive to bitter. He never said those areas were the only places you could taste them. Later, in the 1940s, a psychologist named Edwin Boring re-illustrated Hänig’s data in a way that exaggerated these tiny differences into exclusive zones. That simplified picture made its way into textbooks, and the myth stuck for generations. ### What actually happens Your tongue is covered with tiny bumps called papillae, and most of them contain clusters of taste buds. Each taste bud holds 50–100 taste receptor cells, and those cells can respond to **all five basic tastes** (including umami, discovered later). So no matter where a taste bud is—tip, side, or back—it can detect sweet, salty, sour, bitter, or umami. There are, however, genuine subtle variations in sensitivity across the tongue. For instance, the back of the tongue has a higher density of receptors for bitterness, which may be an evolutionary advantage—bitter can signal poison, and having extra sensitivity at the back might trigger a gag reflex if you’ve already swallowed something dangerous. But you can absolutely taste sugar or salt back there, and you can taste bitter with the tip. So next time someone pulls out the old tongue map, you can share that the real story is more interesting: your whole tongue is a team player when it comes to flavor.
