y on inner ear structures and proprioceptive feedback from the feet. The tongue map would be the equivalent of saying *only* the cerebellum does balance, which isn't true. ### Why the brain analogy feels so similar I think the reason your analogy feels so compelling is that everyday language smooths over this distinction. We talk about the "visual cortex" and the "sweet tip" with the same casual phrasing. But underneath, the biology is qualitatively different. The visual cortex is an anatomical region where visual signals are reconstructed into images—it's a *processing center*. The tip of the tongue is a region with a slightly higher density of sweet receptors—it's a *sensitivity hotspot* on a continuous carpet of mixed detectors. It's the difference between a power plant and a hill that gets more sun. ### What would make the tongue map correct If the tongue map were true in the strong brain-like sense, this would have to hold: anesthetize the tip of your tongue, and you stop tasting sugar entirely; anesthetize the back, and bitter vanishes. That's easily testable, and it fails every time. You can taste sugar anywhere, just fainter. That's not how the visual cortex works. So your brain analogy is spot-on for why the phrasing seems normal, and it highlights exactly what's at stake. In neuroscience, functional maps are often about *necessary* regions. The old tongue map falsely claimed that kind of exclusivity for what are really just subtle, overlapping gradients. That's the core distinction—and it's one your own experience with food actually supports, since you can absolutely taste a little bitterness on the tip if you pay attention, just not as vividly.
