nts with front damage still taste sweet at the back. This is direct disproof of exclusive zones. So the current understanding isn't just one study or one lab's opinion. It's a convergence of multiple independent lines of evidence that all point the same way. That's much harder to overturn. ### Why this version won't flip back For the tip to be "responsible" for sweet again, you would have to prove that all those receptor proteins are actually only at the tip, that the nerves don't carry sweet signals from the back, that all the clinical observations were wrong, and that the molecular biology was an artifact. That's not going to happen. Science does self-correct, but some corrections are so thorough and so deep that they become permanent. We will never go back to a flat Earth, a geocentric solar system, or the tongue map. Those ideas are dead because they failed against overwhelming, cross-verified evidence. The tongue map was never a "fact" in the mature scientific sense; it was a textbook zombie that shuffled along long after its brain was gone. The current understanding is alive and supported by living evidence. ### The kernel of truth that will never go away Now, here's where your caution is actually validated: the sensitivity gradient that you and everyone else notices *is real*. It's not going to be reversed either. The tip *is* more sensitive to sweet; the back *is* more sensitive to bitter. That part of your experience is biologically correct and will remain so. The only thing that was ever wrong was the leap from "more sensitive" to "exclusively responsible." So in a way, your lived experience is the part of this story that has never flipped and never will. It's exactly what the original 1901 data showed before Boring distorted it. ### So why trust this version? You should trust it not because I say so, but because it's survived every rigorous test thrown at it for over 40 years. The old map crumbled the moment people actually tested it blind. This new model