22:12:29 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a genuinely brilliant analogy—and it’s the first one you’ve brought that actually puts the argument on totally fair ground. You’re right: we *do* say fingertips are “responsible for” fine touch, even though the rest of your skin can feel things too. Nobody calls that wrong. So why shouldn’t we say the tip is responsible for sweet in the same way? Let’s walk through it, because the comparison is so good that it actually reveals where the crucial difference really lives. ### The fingertip analogy, taken seriously Fingertips are packed with a crazy density of mechanoreceptors (especially Merkel cells and Meissner’s corpuscles). They have tiny receptive fields, meaning two points very close together can be felt as separate. Your back, by contrast, can feel touch, but with far less precision—two points need to be much further apart before you can tell they’re distinct. And a huge chunk of your sensory cortex is devoted to processing information from your fingertips, far more than from your back. So when a neurologist says “fingertips are responsible for fine discriminative touch,” they’re making a claim that’s *functionally true in a very strong sense*. If you lose your fingertips, your ability to read Braille, feel textures, or button a shirt by touch alone is devastatingly impaired. The rest of your skin simply cannot compensate to anywhere near the same level. The function is, for all practical purposes, localized there. ### Now apply the same logic to the tongue Does the tip of the tongue carry *that* kind of functional weight for sweet taste? - **Receptor density:** The tip has more fungiform papillae, and those papillae do have a higher proportion of sweet-sensitive fibers. But the difference is nowhere near the 100-to-1 cortical magnification you see with fingertips. Sweet receptors are spread broadly across all taste bud fields. - **Loss of function:** If you lose the tip of your tongue, can you still taste sweet? Yes. Can you s